A great game, just fundamentally flawed in its own identity. This game thinks it's an open world adventure, but it's not. Played linearly, this is a great story, in a nice setting and a top notch sountrack. Play from mission to mission, absorb the atmosphere, listen to real world hits and collect real world Playboy magazines. Thumbs up!
However, do not approach it as an open world, in the same way you might play GTA or Saints Row, etc. For one thing, you're never just let loose in the city with nothing to do, you're always in a story chapter with a mission (occasionally that mission may be to make a certain amount of money, but is still a mission). The open world elements are garbage:
- put gas in your car;
- rob identical shops;
- steal and crush or export cars;
- collect 159 wanted posters that are in obscure locations with nothing in game to indicate where they might be, except physically looking around the entire city (on the roof of an unclimbable building, only reachable by jumping off a freeway? Come on!);
The DLC is half-arsed and veers towards open world instead of story-driven, which is a shame, they're basically unplayable. Very little story/cutscenes, instead most missions are simply a short text briefing over some concept art, and the missions are boring, identikit generic pieces, ranging from shoot guys, destroy stuff, steal cars, or all three at once, with the added bonus of being arcadey, hi-score chasing and on a timer. I couldn't bear playing more than 25% of each: the two Jimmy pieces, and Joe's adventures.
Instead, embrace the main story. Play it at Christmas, the atmosphere is unbeatable. The characters are mostly well voiced and the story, whilst being a bit of a generic gangster romp, is worth sticking with. The driving, shooting, even the stealth are substantial enough and enjoyable, even at the hardest difficulty (for the most part). Grab it cheap, play the story and move on.

